What the candidates are, and why they are turned away

Run 564's panel said w8m3.  The boot thread is where run 561 said it was --
between vm_set_restrictions() and bsd_init(), in kperf_init_early()'s wait
for every CPU to be accounted for, a count that only IOKit publishes.  The
matching thread was inside probeCandidates, and none of D1 to D5 was on the
panel.  D5 is written at the end of every candidate that reaches the loop's
body, so every candidate was turned away before the body.

Where they are turned away is not on the panel: the reasons live in
NIO_DROP, and the summary that prints them goes through printf, into the
console ring, which is where the boot stopped.  The drop reasons themselves
are drawn on the panel by another patch of this series, but these two drops
happen before that macro is reached, so they leave no mark at all.

So the panel is told directly.  C is the number of candidates the call was
given, F the class of each of them, N that a candidate was a notifier and X
that it was not a dictionary -- the two ways a candidate leaves without
reaching the body and without a reason.

